        {
          "post_id": "488320",
          "text": "<p>Consider the directed, weighted graph G defined by the following vertices and edges:</p><ul><li>Vertices: $\\{A, B, C, D, E, F\\}$</li><li>Edges and Weights:</li><li>$\\mathrm{A} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{B}(4)$</li><li>$\\mathrm{A} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{C}(2)$</li><li>$\\mathrm{B} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{C}(5)$</li><li>$\\mathrm{B} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{D}(10)$</li><li>$C \\rightarrow E$ ( 3 )</li><li>$\\mathrm{C} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{D}$ (8)</li><li>$D \\rightarrow F$ (11)</li><li>$\\mathrm{E} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{D}(4)$</li><li>$\\mathrm{E} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{F}(10)$</li></ul><p>Using Dijkstra's algorithm, what is the cost of the single-source shortest path from vertex $\\mathbf{A}$ to vertex F?</p><ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\"><li>$21$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$20$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$15$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$25$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "C",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "subject": "Algorithms",
          "day": "Day 91"
        },
        {
          "post_id": "488317",
          "text": "<p>Consider the following three functions:</p><ul><li>$f(n)=\\left(\\log _2 n\\right)^{\\log _2 n}$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$g(n)=n^{\\sqrt{\\log _2 n}}$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$h(n)=(\\sqrt{n})$ !</li></ul><p>Which of the following statements about their asymptotic growth is true?</p><ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\"><li>$f(n)$ is $\\Omega(g(n))$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$h(n)$ is $O(g(n))$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$g(n)$ is $O(f(n))$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$g(n)$ is $O(h(n))$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "D",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "subject": "Algorithms",
          "day": "Day 91"
        },
        {
          "post_id": "488321",
          "text": "<p>In a directed acyclic graph (DAG) with a source vertex $s$, the quality-score of a directed path is defined to be the sum of the weights of the edges on the path. For any other vertex v , its quality-score is the maximum quality-score among all possible paths from $s$ to $v$. The qualityscore of $s$ is 0 .<br>&nbsp;</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img width=\"109\" height=\"250\" alt=\"\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"></p><p><br>What is the sum of the quality-scores of all vertices on the graph?&nbsp;</p>",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "19",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "subject": "Algorithms",
          "day": "Day 91"
        },
        {
          "post_id": "488316",
          "text": "<p>Consider the following two functions, designed to test a deep understanding of asymptotic behavior:</p><p>$$<br>\\begin{gathered}<br>f_1(n)= \\begin{cases}(n!)^2 & \\text { for } 0 \\leq n \\leq 40 \\\\<br>n^2 \\log n & \\text { for } n&gt;40\\end{cases} \\\\<br>f_2(n)= \\begin{cases}100 n^3 & \\text { for } 0 \\leq n \\leq 1,000,000 \\\\<br>n^2 \\log ^2 n & \\text { for } n&gt;1,000,000\\end{cases}<br>\\end{gathered}<br>$$</p><p><br>Which of the following statements correctly describes the long-term growth relationship between these two functions?</p><ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\"><li>$f_1(n)$ is $\\Theta\\left(f_2(n)\\right)$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$f_1(n)$ is $\\Omega\\left(f_2(n)\\right)$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$f_2(n)$ is $\\Omega\\left(f_1(n)\\right)$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$f_1(n)$ is $O\\left(n^2\\right)$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "C",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "subject": "Algorithms",
          "day": "Day 91"
        },
        {
          "post_id": "488319",
          "text": "<p>Consider a state space of positive integers from 1 to 100 , where the start state is 1. The successor function for a state numbered $n$ returns two states: $n * 2$ and $n + 3$. Any successor state greater than 100 is considered invalid and is ignored.</p><p>Assume that for any state, the valid successor $n * 2$ is explored before $n+3$, and that previously expanded states are not revisited.</p><p>Which ONE of the following statements about breadth-first search (BFS) and depth-first search (DFS) is true when reaching the goal state number 10 ?</p><ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\"><li>BFS EXPANDS MORE STATES THAN DFS.<br>&nbsp;</li><li>DFS EXPANDS MORE STATES THAN BFS.<br>&nbsp;</li><li>BOTH BFS AND DFS EXPAND AN EQUAL NUMBER OF STATES.<br>&nbsp;</li><li>NEITHER BFS NOR DFS CAN REACH THE GOAL STATE NUMBER 10.</li></ol>",
